<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Chachapoya</h1><p class="st-meta">800 CE – 1475 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Peoples of the cloud forests of northern Peru, builders of the great walled citadel of Kuélap and of cliffside tombs and sarcophagi.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Chachapoya culture developed from around 800 in the montane forests between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maranon-river">Marañón</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huallaga-river">Huallaga</a> rivers, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazonas-region">Amazonas</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. Communities built circular stone houses on ridges and grew into a network of fortified settlements.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Chachapoya were not a single state but a confederation of chiefdoms sharing culture and allying in war. Their most famous site, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuelap">Kuélap</a>, is a walled platform up to 20 metres high containing hundreds of round buildings. They placed their dead in cliffside mausoleums such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revash">Revash</a> and in tall clay sarcophagi, the purunmachus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karajia">Karajía</a>. Their stone buildings carry zigzag and rhomboid friezes.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a> conquered the Chachapoya around 1475 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-inca-yupanqui">Túpac Inca Yupanqui</a>, facing repeated revolts and deporting many people. Some Chachapoya allied with the Spanish after 1532. Epidemics and colonial rule then depopulated the region, and many sites remained hidden in forest until modern times.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/chachapoya.jpg" alt="Chachapoya" width="258" height="367"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mapa_cultura_chachapoyas.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 CE – 1475 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancestor worship</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Chacha</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chachapoyas, Warriors of the Clouds</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1138&amp;focus=chachapoya" aria-label="Chachapoya on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2680 2661 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Chachapoya lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3027 2937h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3000 2917h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M3000 2917h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Peru. <a href="/atlas?year=1138&amp;focus=chachapoya">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

